><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250502164831+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250502164835+10'00'" name="c638a4ca-1fde-4ef2-834e-afe62a220ab3" page="4" coords="90.973200,459.330000,330.565000,459.326000,90.973200,449.471000,330.565000,449.467000,79.020700,448.869000,330.565000,448.865000,79.020700,439.010000,330.565000,439.006000,79.020800,438.408000,330.563000,438.404000,79.020800,428.549000,330.563000,428.545000,79.018900,427.947000,330.566000,427.943000,79.018900,418.088000,330.566000,418.085000,79.021200,417.487000,330.565000,417.483000,79.021200,407.628000,330.565000,407.624000,79.020700,407.026000,330.565000,407.022000,79.020700,397.167000,330.565000,397.163000,79.020200,396.565000,330.564000,396.561000,79.020200,386.706000,330.564000,386.702000,79.019500,386.104000,330.564000,386.100000,79.019500,376.245000,330.564000,376.241000,79.020200,375.643000,330.564000,375.639000,79.020200,365.785000,330.564000,365.781000,79.019900,365.183000,330.564000,365.179000,79.019900,355.324000,330.564000,355.320000,79.020100,354.722000,330.564000,354.718000,79.020100,344.863000,330.564000,344.859000,79.019200,344.261000,330.564000,344.257000,79.019200,334.402000,330.564000,334.398000,79.019900,333.800000,330.565000,333.796000,79.019900,323.942000,330.565000,323.937000,126.335000,324.264000,138.583000,324.264000,126.335000,316.870000,138.583000,316.870000,79.020400,323.340000,126.339000,323.340000,79.020400,313.481000,126.339000,313.481000" rect="78.710800,313.173000,330.874000,459.638000" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This study examined core temperature recovery and the poten-tial impact of each component of human heat balance in athleteswith TP, HP and LP compared to AB individuals following exercise inthe heat. Importantly, these data highlight a potential ‘worst-case’scenario whereby athletes remain in the heat following exerciseand cooling strategies are not readily available. In addition, thesefindings are limited to athletes with a complete SCI and certain ath-letes with TP who have an incomplete SCI. Individuals with greatersympathetic innervation due to a more incomplete SCI may expe-rience less of a delay in core temperature recovery. Finally, futureresearch exploring post-exercise thermoregulatory responses infemales with a SCI is warranted to examine whether any sex dif-ferences are present, such as those previously observed in ABpopulations.</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-8.0pt;text-align:left;color:#007FAB;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>26,2</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="4" rect="637.180000,367.330000,821.180000,459.330000"
/></highlight
